      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, let's determine the total number of dishes on the menu. Given that four meatless dishes represent one fifth of the menu, we can calculate the total number of dishes as follows:\n\\[\n\\text{Total dishes} = 4 \\times 5 = 20\n\\]\nNext, we know that three of these meatless dishes contain gluten. Since we need to find out how many of the total dishes can be eaten without both meat and gluten, we must first exclude these three dishes from our consideration:\n\\[\n\\text{Dishes without meat} = 4\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Dishes without meat and gluten} = 4 - 3 = 1\n\\]\nThus, all four vegetarian dishes can be consumed due to their absence of meat. However, only one specific category (meatless, gluten-free) contains dishes that are genuinely acceptable for our vegetarian friend. As per the problem statement, three of those four vegetarian dishes contain gluten, indicating a discrepancy:\n\\[\n\\text{Total edible dishes} = \\text{Dishes without meat} = 4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the fraction of the items on the menu that my friend can eat is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{Total edible dishes}}{\\text{Total dishes}} = \\frac{4}{20} = \\frac{1}{5}\n\\]\n\nSo, the fraction of the items on the menu that can be consumed by the vegetarian friend is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{5}}\n\\]",
